11:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L18",null,{"guide":{"date":"May 5, 1864","place":"Saunders Field, Orange Turnpike, Orange Plank Road, and the Brock Road intersection, Virginia","result":"Grant and Meade initially believed they faced only part of Lee’s army, partly because U.S. cavalry had not located the Confederate columns. Warren attacked Ewell at Saunders Field before his flanks were covered; the attack broke down in the woods and was repulsed. Three miles south, Getty reached the Brock Road intersection just before Heth and stopped the Confederate advance. Getty then attacked alone shortly before 4 p.m. while Hancock’s II Corps was still arriving","facts":[{"label":"Distance Wadsworth crossed","value":"About 1 mile in 4 hours"},{"label":"Saunders Field attack","value":"9 brigades at about 1 p.m."},{"label":"Attack failed by","value":"About 2:30 p.m."},{"label":"Distance between Ewell and Hill","value":"Nearly 3 miles"},{"label":"Hancock’s column depth","value":"About 6 miles"},{"label":"Getty’s attacking force","value":"About 6,000 men in 3 brigades"},{"label":"First Confederate volley range","value":"About 75 yards"},{"label":"Two main battle sectors","value":"Orange Turnpike and Orange Plank Road"}],"maps":[{"title":"Saunders Field and Brock Road, noon–3 p.m.","caption":"The National Park Service map shows Warren’s V Corps attacking Ewell at Saunders Field while Heth moves east on Orange Plank Road toward Getty’s position at the Brock Road intersection.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/media/photo/gallery-item.htm?gid=F6B164DB-01BE-4E4E-A97CC65D8F0FD22E&id=17fa8c32-a2c7-4633-92e2-d76770d6fee4","image":"/images/wilderness-may5-noon-3pm-map.jpg","alt":"Detailed troop movement map of the Battle of the Wilderness from noon to 3 p.m. on May 5, 1864"},{"title":"The two battle sectors, 3–7 p.m.","caption":"The second map shows continued fighting at Saunders Field and the separate attacks around the Brock Road–Orange Plank Road intersection. Wilcox moves north to reconnect Hill with Ewell.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/media/photo/gallery-item.htm?gid=F6B164DB-01BE-4E4E-A97CC65D8F0FD22E&id=56bdecb3-562f-4819-8d2e-0a13c9032d61","image":"/images/wilderness-may5-3pm-7pm-map.jpg","alt":"Detailed troop movement map of the Battle of the Wilderness from 3 to 7 p.m. on May 5, 1864"},{"title":"The battlefield today","caption":"The modern map shows Orange Turnpike to the north, Orange Plank Road to the south, and Brock Road connecting the U.S. positions. Saunders Field lies along the turnpike west of Wilderness Tavern.","source":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright","embed":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/export/embed.html?bbox=-77.79%2C38.27%2C-77.66%2C38.36&layer=mapnik&marker=38.3138%2C-77.7592"},{"title":"The Wilderness in the Overland Campaign","caption":"The broader map places the fighting west of Fredericksburg after Grant crossed the Rapidan and before the movement to Spotsylvania.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99448882/","image":"/images/overland-campaign-1864-map.jpg","alt":"Historical map showing the Wilderness in the Overland Campaign"}],"moments":[{"time":"1:27","audioTime":87,"title":"The armies made contact on May 5","detail":"Ewell’s corps met U.S. troops after Grant’s army crossed the Rapidan and entered the Wilderness."},{"time":"1:50","audioTime":110,"title":"Meade underestimated the Confederate force","detail":"Initial reports described a small enemy body, leading Meade and Grant to order an attack against what they believed was an isolated part of Lee’s army."},{"time":"3:02","audioTime":182,"title":"Sheridan’s cavalry had not found Lee’s columns","detail":"Orders called for scouting on the main roads, but James Wilson mostly picketed them and withdrew as U.S. infantry arrived."},{"time":"4:03","audioTime":243,"title":"Wilson operated on the wrong road","detail":"Most of his division moved near Catharpin Road, the one route Confederate infantry was not using, and skirmished with cavalry instead of locating Hill and Ewell."},{"time":"5:30","audioTime":330,"title":"Ewell formed at Saunders Field","detail":"Edward Johnson and Robert Rodes held the front line behind fieldworks while Jubal Early’s division remained in reserve."},{"time":"6:04","audioTime":364,"title":"Warren prepared the V Corps attack","detail":"Charles Griffin would attack west, John Robinson remained mostly in reserve, and James Wadsworth and Samuel Crawford were ordered toward Griffin’s left."},{"time":"7:14","audioTime":434,"title":"The Wilderness delayed Wadsworth","detail":"His division took four hours to cross roughly one mile of thickets, vines, fallen timber, hills, and swampy ground."},{"time":"8:13","audioTime":493,"title":"Crawford refused to leave the Tuning farm","detail":"He saw A. P. Hill approaching on Orange Plank Road and feared that moving north would uncover the Brock Road intersection and isolate Hancock."},{"time":"9:12","audioTime":552,"title":"U.S. commanders recognized Lee’s strength","detail":"Reports placed Hill on Orange Plank Road and Confederate cavalry north of Orange Turnpike, showing that more than an isolated force was advancing."},{"time":"10:01","audioTime":601,"title":"Meade ordered the army to consolidate","detail":"He sent George Getty’s VI Corps division to hold the critical Brock Road–Orange Plank Road intersection."},{"time":"10:53","audioTime":653,"title":"Warren found Griffin’s right exposed","detail":"The Confederate line extended farther north than expected, and Horatio Wright’s VI Corps division had not arrived to protect the flank."},{"time":"12:09","audioTime":729,"title":"Meade ordered an immediate attack","detail":"Warren and Griffin preferred to wait, but Meade directed them to advance before all supporting formations were in place."},{"time":"12:21","audioTime":741,"title":"Nine brigades entered the woods","detail":"Griffin and Wadsworth advanced, but the terrain quickly broke their lines into disconnected groups."},{"time":"13:18","audioTime":798,"title":"The attack began around 1 p.m.","detail":"Confederate volleys met Griffin in Saunders Field while Wadsworth’s troops struggled to keep direction and contact south of the clearing."},{"time":"14:16","audioTime":856,"title":"Wadsworth’s division came apart","detail":"One brigade exposed its flank, the Iron Brigade broke after a counterattack, and another brigade became stuck in swampy ground. Within 40 minutes the division no longer operated as a unit."},{"time":"15:19","audioTime":919,"title":"Bartlett briefly broke the Confederate line","detail":"Supported by two guns on Orange Turnpike, Joseph Bartlett’s brigade crossed Saunders Field and captured part of the works but withdrew because adjacent brigades had not broken through."},{"time":"16:03","audioTime":963,"title":"Fire spread across the battlefield","detail":"Dry brush and burning fieldworks ignited the woods. Wounded soldiers unable to move were caught by the flames."},{"time":"18:54","audioTime":1134,"title":"The Saunders Field attack had failed","detail":"By about 2:30 p.m., Warren’s unsupported attack was over. Griffin blamed the failure on the absence of support on both flanks."},{"time":"20:49","audioTime":1249,"title":"Hill advanced on Orange Plank Road","detail":"Three miles south of Saunders Field, Heth led Hill’s corps east through Parker’s Store toward the Brock Road intersection."},{"time":"21:56","audioTime":1316,"title":"Getty’s infantry blocked the road","detail":"Meade’s reinforcement reached the intersection and stopped Heth after some Confederates had approached within roughly 30 yards."},{"time":"23:24","audioTime":1404,"title":"Lee reviewed the separated corps","detail":"Hill’s advance placed his corps nearly three miles south and east of Ewell, creating a dangerous gap between the two Confederate columns."},{"time":"24:18","audioTime":1458,"title":"U.S. skirmishers approached Tapp Field","detail":"Crawford’s men briefly emerged north of Lee’s headquarters. Lee then sent Wilcox north from Parker’s Store to reestablish contact with Ewell."},{"time":"25:12","audioTime":1512,"title":"Getty built defenses at the crossroads","detail":"His division held for roughly two hours while Hancock’s II Corps marched toward the intersection."},{"time":"26:22","audioTime":1582,"title":"Meade repeatedly redirected Hancock","detail":"Hancock received several changing orders to countermarch, join Getty, and attack Hill while his divisions were spread along six miles of roads."},{"time":"27:30","audioTime":1650,"title":"II Corps arrived slowly","detail":"Hancock and his staff reached Brock Road by about 1 p.m., but only two divisions arrived during the next hour and the others remained farther back."},{"time":"28:05","audioTime":1685,"title":"Meade ordered Getty forward before 4 p.m.","detail":"Getty obeyed the direct order rather than wait for Hancock to assemble a larger force."},{"time":"28:41","audioTime":1721,"title":"Getty attacked with about 6,000 men","detail":"Three VI Corps brigades left their breastworks and advanced west through dense woods without knowing the exact Confederate position."},{"time":"29:17","audioTime":1757,"title":"A volley struck at about 75 yards","detail":"Heth’s line fired repeated volleys into Getty’s troops, who could see only fire and smoke through the vegetation."},{"time":"30:51","audioTime":1851,"title":"Getty’s division held in place","detail":"The terrain prevented a rapid bayonet charge. The U.S. line could not advance but remained engaged while Hancock prepared his attack."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"#315f8c","branches":[{"commander":"Ulysses S. Grant","command":"General-in-chief","subordinates":"Approved Meade’s decision to attack what appeared to be an isolated Confederate force"},{"commander":"George G. Meade","command":"Army of the Potomac","subordinates":"Directed Warren at Saunders Field and sent Getty and Hancock to Brock Road"},{"commander":"Gouverneur K. Warren","command":"V Corps","subordinates":"Charles Griffin, James Wadsworth, John Robinson, and Samuel Crawford"},{"commander":"John Sedgwick","command":"VI Corps","subordinates":"George Getty and Horatio Wright"},{"commander":"Winfield Scott Hancock","command":"II Corps","subordinates":"Marched north to reinforce Getty at Brock Road"},{"commander":"Philip H. Sheridan","command":"Cavalry Corps","subordinates":"James Wilson’s division failed to locate the main Confederate columns"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"#9a3d32","branches":[{"commander":"Robert E. Lee","command":"Army of Northern Virginia","subordinates":"Advanced Ewell and Hill on parallel roads and worked to reconnect them"},{"commander":"Richard S. Ewell","command":"Second Corps","subordinates":"Edward Johnson and Robert Rodes in front; Jubal Early in reserve"},{"commander":"A. P. Hill","command":"Third Corps","subordinates":"Henry Heth led on Orange Plank Road; Cadmus Wilcox followed"},{"commander":"Henry Heth","command":"Hill’s lead division","subordinates":"Reached the Brock Road intersection and met Getty’s division"},{"commander":"Cadmus Wilcox","command":"Hill’s second division","subordinates":"Sent north to close the gap with Ewell"}]}],"people":[{"name":"Gouverneur K. Warren","role":"Directed the V Corps attack at Saunders Field despite exposed flanks","side":"United States"},{"name":"Charles Griffin","role":"Led the division that briefly broke Ewell’s line in Saunders Field","side":"United States"},{"name":"James Wadsworth","role":"Led a division through the woods south of Saunders Field, where it lost cohesion","side":"United States"},{"name":"George Getty","role":"Seized the Brock Road intersection and attacked Heth before Hancock was ready","side":"United States"},{"name":"Philip H. Sheridan","role":"Controlled the cavalry screen that failed to identify Lee’s principal columns before the battle","side":"United States"},{"name":"Robert E. Lee","role":"Advanced Ewell and Hill into the Wilderness and tried to close the gap between them","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/robert-e-lee-1864.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666540/"},{"personId":"ulysses-s-grant","role":"Approved the decision to attack before the Confederate deployment was fully understood"}],"losses":[],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/wilderness-may5-noon-3pm-map.jpg","alt":"National Park Service map of the first afternoon at the Wilderness","caption":"This map shows why the fighting became two separate battles along Orange Turnpike and Orange Plank Road.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/media/photo/gallery-item.htm?gid=F6B164DB-01BE-4E4E-A97CC65D8F0FD22E&id=17fa8c32-a2c7-4633-92e2-d76770d6fee4"},{"image":"/images/ulysses-s-grant.jpg","alt":"Portrait of Ulysses S. Grant","caption":"Grant approved an attack based on incomplete information about the size and location of Lee’s army.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666428/"},{"image":"/images/robert-e-lee-1864.jpg","alt":"Portrait of Robert E. Lee","caption":"Lee sent Ewell and Hill east on parallel roads, producing the two main combat sectors.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666540/"}],"sources":[{"label":"Civil War Podcast episode 481","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/2025/01/19/civil-war-podcast-episode-481/"},{"label":"National Park Service: History of the Battle of the Wilderness","url":"https://www.nps.gov/frsp/learn/historyculture/wildspot.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Wilderness troop movement maps","url":"https://www.nps.gov/media/photo/gallery.htm?id=F6B164DB-01BE-4E4E-A97CC65D8F0FD22E"},{"label":"National Park Service: Saunders Field","url":"https://www.nps.gov/places/saunders-field.htm"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Overland Campaign map","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99448882/"}]}}]}]
